						<div  class="elementor-container elementor-column-gap-default">
					<div class="elementor-column elementor-col-100 elementor-top-column elementor-element elementor-element-a3046fb" data-id="a3046fb" data-element_type="column" data-e-type="column">
			<div class="elementor-widget-wrap elementor-element-populated">
						<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-dd00f3a elementor-widget elementor-widget-heading" data-id="dd00f3a" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-settings="{&quot;_animation&quot;:&quot;none&quot;}" data-widget_type="heading.default">
					<h1 class="elementor-heading-title elementor-size-default">The Ultimate Guide For Pregnant Women Who Are Involved In Car Accidents</h1>				</div>
				<section class="elementor-section elementor-inner-section elementor-element elementor-element-40065ba elementor-section-full_width elementor-section-height-default elementor-section-height-default" data-id="40065ba" data-element_type="section" data-e-type="section">
						<div class="elementor-container elementor-column-gap-wide">
					<div class="elementor-column elementor-col-100 elementor-inner-column elementor-element elementor-element-8ef1f4d" data-id="8ef1f4d" data-element_type="column" data-e-type="column">
			<div class="elementor-widget-wrap elementor-element-populated">
						<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-8d468c8 post-main-contents elementor-widget elementor-widget-theme-post-content" data-id="8d468c8" data-element_type="widget" data-e-type="widget" data-widget_type="theme-post-content.default">
					<blockquote>
<p style="text-align: justify;"><b>TL;DR:</b> A minor car accident during pregnancy can harm your baby by causing placental abruption, oxygen loss, or early labor even without visible injuries. The at-fault driver may be liable for medical costs and complications, and delayed symptoms can weaken claims if care and documentation are not obtained quickly.</p>
</blockquote>
<div class="atf-highlights">
<h6 class="atf-highlights__title">Highlights:</h6>
<ul>
<li>Get an ER evaluation within 24 hours, even if you feel fine.</li>
<li>Schedule OB-GYN follow-up and ask about ultrasound, NST, and observation.</li>
<li>Track warning signs: bleeding, contractions, low movement, dizziness, headaches, swelling.</li>
<li>Photograph vehicles and injuries, and file a police report promptly.</li>
<li>Save every bill, discharge summary, prescription, and work-absence note.</li>
<li>Tell insurers only crash facts; decline recorded statements without counsel.</li>
<li>Calendar filing limits: <b>2 years</b> for injury, <b>6 months</b> for government claims.</li>
</ul>
<p class="atf-highlights__note"><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep a dated symptom and appointment log, and stick to facts when communicating with insurers.</p>
</div>
		<div data-elementor-type="section" data-elementor-id="233058" class="elementor elementor-233058 elementor-location-single" data-elementor-post-type="elementor_library">
			<div class="elementor-element elementor-element-1357f3c e-ecs-flex e-flex e-con-boxed e-con e-parent" data-id="1357f3c" data-element_type="container" data-e-type="container" data-settings="{&quot;ecs_container_type&quot;:&quot;flex&quot;}">
